#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""grok-cloud: Midjourney and Suno for every agent, through Grok's cloud computer (Boss, 2026-10-19).

Grok's cloud computer, in his Grok app, is signed in to Midjourney and Suno; Grok's CLI can't reach it. Grok runs a
"routine" with a webhook instead: this service sends it jobs and takes the results back. It runs on the Mac Pro.

  Jobs, on the tailnet only (port 8493, owner's devices only, checked with `tailscale whois`):
    POST /v1/jobs      {"tool": "midjourney"|"suno", "kind": "image"|"video"|"song", "prompt": "...", "session": "..."}
                       -> {"job": ID, "status": "sent"}; the file arrives later: in ~/Soma/made/DATE, in the Pocket,
                       and as agent mail to the device (and session) that asked.
    GET  /v1/jobs/ID   that job's status; GET /v1/jobs the latest ones.
  Results, from the internet through Tailscale Funnel (port 8494 on loopback, mounted at /grok/cb):
    POST /grok/cb/ID/TOKEN   Grok's answer: JSON {"status", "message", "files": [https links]} or multipart with
                             `file` parts. TOKEN is made per job and works once; anything else gets 404.

The webhook's URL and sender key are in the login Keychain (service soma-grok-webhook, accounts url and key), never in
a file; how the key is sent (a header) is in ~/.config/soma/grok-cloud.json, which holds no secret. At most
JOBS_PER_HOUR and JOBS_PER_DAY jobs are sent, so a runaway agent can't spend his Midjourney and Suno credits.

  grok-cloud serve | grok-cloud install (launchd) | grok-cloud test-config
"""
